H("3. Results")
H("3.1 Variance structure (RQ1)", 2)
P(f"Between-journal differences explained {pc(vc['sigma2_journal']/tot)} of the variance of ln D (95% CI {pc(vci['icc_journal'][0])}–{pc(vci['icc_journal'][1])}); year-to-year differences within a journal added {pc(vc['sigma2_year']/tot)}, and {pc(vc['sigma2_resid']/tot)} was residual between-article variation (Table 1, Figure 2). H1 (journal share below 50%) was supported. The non-frame PubMed journals gave a similar journal share ({pc(vce['icc_journal'])}, {pc(vcie['icc_journal'][0])}–{pc(vcie['icc_journal'][1])}; {vce['n_journals']} journals).")
t2 = pd.DataFrame([["Between journals", vc["sigma2_journal"], vc["sigma2_journal"]/tot], ["Journal × acceptance year", vc["sigma2_year"], vc["sigma2_year"]/tot], ["Residual (between articles)", vc["sigma2_resid"], vc["sigma2_resid"]/tot]], columns=["Component", "Variance of ln D", "Share"])
table(t2, "Table 1. Variance components of ln acceptance time (800 journals, acceptance years 2021–2025).")
fig(F + "fig_variance_components.png", "Figure 2. Share of the variance of ln D by component. Whiskers are not drawn; the 95% CI of the journal share is in the panel title.", 3.0)
H("3.2 Out-of-time accuracy and calibration (RQ2)", 2)
P(f"The evaluation covered {M['n_journal_origins']:,} journal-origins from {M['n_journals_forecast']} journals and {M['n_target_papers']:,} target papers. Journal-specific forecasts clearly beat the publisher-pooled forecast (F0 minus F2 pinball difference {con['F0 - F2']['mean_diff']:+.4f}, 95% CI {con['F0 - F2']['ci_lo']:+.4f} to {con['F0 - F2']['ci_hi']:+.4f}; skill {pc(fm['F0']['skill'])}; Table 2, Figure 3). Differences between the journal-specific forecasters were small. The simple empirical forecaster (F0) outperformed the lognormal (F3) and the empirical-Bayes lognormal (F4); F4 was worse than F0 in all three size strata (significantly in the two larger ones, {st['150-400']['n_journals']} and {st['>=400']['n_journals']} journals, Holm-adjusted p < 0.001 and {st['>=400']['p_holm']:.3f}; borderline in the {st['<150']['n_journals']} journals with fewer than 150 visible papers, p = {st['<150']['p_holm']:.3f}), so H3 was not supported. Because the eligibility rule (at least 100 visible papers) removes the small-sample regime where shrinkage should matter, and because a lognormal is a poor description of these data, this is not a general test of shrinkage: an exploratory shrinkage of the empirical quantiles themselves (F7) and a six-month persistence forecaster (F8) also failed to improve on F0 in any stratum (overall difference F7 − F0 {M['rev']['R3_overall']['F7']:+.4f}, F8 − F0 {M['rev']['R3_overall']['F8']:+.4f}); only {M['rev']['R3_strata'][0]['n_journals']} journals had 30–99 visible papers, too few for a conclusion in that regime. Time-decayed weighting (F6) was marginally better than F0 and a 24-month window (F1) marginally worse; all these differences were below 2% of loss (Table 3).")
tab2 = pd.read_csv(f"{ROOT}/tables/table3_forecasters.csv"); tab2 = tab2.fillna("–")
tab2.columns = ["Forecaster", "Journals", "Mean pinball", "Skill vs F2", "Coverage of 80% interval − 0.80 [95% CI]", "80% width", "Winkler 80%"]
table(tab2, "Table 2. Out-of-time forecast performance (ln-day scale; lower pinball and Winkler are better). F5 is interval-only.")
P(f"Calibration was imperfect in a consistent direction. Nominal 80% intervals from F0 covered {pc(0.8+fm['F0']['cov80_minus'])} of later papers (−{abs(fm['F0']['cov80_minus'])*100:.1f} percentage points; 95% CI −{abs(fm['F0']['ci'][1])*100:.1f} to −{abs(fm['F0']['ci'][0])*100:.1f}); the confidence interval straddles the pre-specified −3-point boundary, so the deviation was not shown to exceed 3 points and H2 was not supported by its pre-specified rule. The top probability-integral-transform decile contained {pc(M['pit_F0_top_decile'])} of later papers (nominal 10%), i.e. more papers than expected were slower than the 90th percentile of the visible past (Figure 4). The lognormal forecasters were closer to nominal coverage on average ({pc(0.8+fm['F4']['cov80_minus'])} for F4) but less sharp in loss terms. The 80% intervals were wide: their mean width on the log scale was {fm['F0']['width80']:.2f}, so the upper limit was on average about {ratio80:.1f} times the lower limit. The recent-holdout conformal adjustment (F5) raised coverage only to {pc(0.8+fm['F5']['cov80_minus'])}, consistent with the exchangeability assumption failing under drift.")
fig([F + "fig_skill.png", F + "fig_coverage.png"], "Figure 3. Left: pinball skill of each forecaster relative to the publisher-pooled forecast (F2), by journal. Right: out-of-time coverage of nominal 80% intervals by journal (dashed line = 0.80). Boxes show quartiles, whiskers 1.5 × IQR.")
fig(F + "fig_pit.png", "Figure 4. Probability-integral-transform histograms (deciles) for F0, F3 and F4 pooled over journal-origins; the dashed line is the uniform expectation.", 4.5)
H("3.3 Why do intervals under-cover? A post-hoc diagnostic", 2)
e10 = M["e10"]
P(f"Training F0 on the visible set gave coverage {e10['visible']['cov80_minus_nominal']*100:+.1f} points (as in Table 2). The oracle complete submission cohort, which uses papers accepted after the origin and is therefore not available to an author, still under-covered, by {e10['sub_complete']['cov80_minus_nominal']*100:+.1f} points (95% CI {e10['sub_complete']['ci95'][0]*100:+.1f} to {e10['sub_complete']['ci95'][1]*100:+.1f}) and lowered pinball loss by {pc(1-e10['sub_complete']['mean_pinball']/e10['visible']['mean_pinball'])}. Roughly half of the shortfall therefore arises because slow papers have not yet been accepted when the forecast is made, and half remains when that information is supplied, which is consistent with change between the training and target cohorts (drift) but could also reflect compositional changes we cannot observe. Finite-sample variability under independent sampling at the observed training sizes would account for only {M['rev']['R4_finite_sample_cov80_minus_nominal_iid']*100:+.1f} points (simulation), and journal ln D rose by {M['rev']['R5_change_2021_to_2025_lnD']:.3f} ln-units ({pc(2.718281828**M['rev']['R5_change_2021_to_2025_lnD']-1)}) between acceptance years 2021 and 2025, so a mild secular slowing is one candidate for the remainder. The shortfall was stable across four origins (2022–2025; F0 coverage {M['rev']['R1_by_origin']['2022-01-01']['cov80_F0']*100-80:+.1f}, {M['rev']['R1_by_origin']['2023-01-01']['cov80_F0']*100-80:+.1f}, {M['rev']['R1_by_origin']['2024-01-01']['cov80_F0']*100-80:+.1f} and {M['rev']['R1_by_origin']['2025-01-01']['cov80_F0']*100-80:+.1f} points; pooled {M['rev']['R1_pooled_F0_cov80_minus_nominal']['est']*100:+.1f}, CI {M['rev']['R1_pooled_F0_cov80_minus_nominal']['lo']*100:+.1f} to {M['rev']['R1_pooled_F0_cov80_minus_nominal']['hi']*100:+.1f}; the 2022 and 2025 origins are exploratory, with truncated windows and shorter follow-up respectively). A tempting shortcut—using only recently submitted papers that have already been accepted—was far worse: coverage {e10['sub_trunc']['cov80_minus_nominal']*100:+.1f} points (CI {e10['sub_trunc']['ci95'][0]*100:+.1f} to {e10['sub_trunc']['ci95'][1]*100:+.1f}) and training medians {pc(1-e10['sub_trunc']['median_ratio_train_to_test_median'],0)} below the realised target medians.")
H("3.4 Drift (RQ3, exploratory)", 2)
P(f"The mean squared year-to-year change in a journal's median ln D was {dr['variance_ratio']:.1f} times the value expected from sampling noise alone (permutation null; {dr['n_pairs']:,} journal-year pairs). The median absolute change was {dr['median_abs_delta']:.2f} ln-units (about {pc(2.718281828**dr['median_abs_delta']-1,0)}); {pc(dr['share_abs_delta_gt_0_2'])} of pairs changed by more than 0.2 ln-units (about 22%) and {pc(M['drift_extra']['share_abs_gt_0_5'])} by more than 0.5. Change over time is therefore a first-order feature of these data, consistent with the coverage shortfall that remains in the oracle diagnostic (Figure 5).")
fig([F + "fig_drift.png", F + "fig_median_forecast_vs_realised.png"], "Figure 5. Left: distribution of year-to-year changes in journal median ln D. Right: forecast median (F0) against the realised median of the target cohort for each journal-origin (log axes; dashed line = identity).")
H("3.5 How often is a 72-day claim supported? (RQ4)", 2)
P(f"In the target cohorts, the realised share of papers accepted within 72 days was at least 0.5 in {g[(72,0.5)]['k']} of {g[(72,0.5)]['n_journal_origins']:,} journal-origins ({pc(g[(72,0.5)]['share'])}; 95% CI {pc(g[(72,0.5)]['cp_lo'])}–{pc(g[(72,0.5)]['cp_hi'])}), at least 0.8 in {g[(72,0.8)]['k']} ({pc(g[(72,0.8)]['share'])}) and at least 0.9 in {g[(72,0.9)]['k']} ({pc(g[(72,0.9)]['share'])}; Table 3). The threshold of 72 days (about ten weeks) is illustrative of the maximum-time criteria used in some policies; 120 and 180 days are reported alongside. H4 (fewer than 5% of journal-origins reaching 0.5) was therefore not supported. Because the two origins of a journal are dependent, we also computed a journal-cluster bootstrap interval for the share reaching 0.5 ({pc(M['post']['thr0.5']['cluster_ci95'][0])}–{pc(M['post']['thr0.5']['cluster_ci95'][1])}) and for the share reaching 0.8 ({pc(M['post']['thr0.8']['cluster_ci95'][0])}–{pc(M['post']['thr0.8']['cluster_ci95'][1])}); {pc(M['post']['thr0.5']['share_journals_any_origin'])} of journals reached 0.5 at one or both origins. A minority of journals did accept most papers within 72 days; by coarse title-keyword coding (exploratory, not validated) they were commonest among chemistry, materials and physics journals ({pc(ft['chemistry/materials/physics']['share_fast'],0)} of {ft['chemistry/materials/physics']['n_journals']}) and clinical-medicine journals ({pc(ft['clinical medicine']['share_fast'],0)} of {ft['clinical medicine']['n_journals']}), and rarer among engineering and computing journals ({pc(ft['engineering/computing']['share_fast'],0)} of {ft['engineering/computing']['n_journals']}) and other or unclassified journals ({pc(ft['other/unclassified']['share_fast'],0)} of {ft['other/unclassified']['n_journals']}). Excluding the six journals whose forecast or realised median was below 30 days, which may reflect re-submission dates, left the shares essentially unchanged ({pc(M['sens_lt30']['share_P72_ge_0_5'])} reaching 0.5). Even so, past evidence overstated future speed at the fast end: for the {hb['n']} journal-origins ({hb['n_journals']} journals; no interval, and subject to selection of extreme forecasts) whose F0 forecast of P(D ≤ 72) was at least 0.8 (mean forecast {pc(hb['forecast'],0)}), the realised share was {pc(hb['realised'],0)}. Brier skill relative to the pooled forecast was {pc(M['brier_skill']['F0']['72'])} for the 72-day event.")
