# ------------------------------------------------------------------ discussion
H("4. Discussion")
P(f"Three findings stand out. First, a journal's own history is genuinely informative but only moderately so: using it cuts forecast loss by about {pc(fm['F0']['skill'],0)} relative to ignoring journal identity within a publisher, yet {pc(vc['sigma2_resid']/tot,0)} of the variance lies between articles, so the resulting 80% intervals span roughly a five-fold range. The variance partition agrees with Björk and Solomon (2013) on a much larger and more recent dataset. Second, forecasts are biased towards optimism about speed: coverage is below nominal, more later papers fall beyond the 90th percentile than expected, and the fastest-looking journals did not keep their pace (which may partly reflect regression to the mean after selecting extreme journals). The post-hoc diagnostic suggests that about half of the coverage shortfall comes from the visible set lacking slow papers that are accepted later, and that half persists even with oracle information, consistent with change over time; the conformal adjustment, which assumes exchangeability, repaired only part of the shortfall. Turnaround statistics computed only from recently submitted papers that have already been accepted are far more optimistic still. Third, the simplest estimators were hard to beat. The lognormal and shrinkage forecasters, which improve small-sample stability in principle, were no better, probably because a normal distribution on ln D is a poor description of the data: in an exploratory check, within-journal ln D was left-skewed (median skewness {M['shape']['median_skew']:.2f}; {pc(M['shape']['share_skew_lt_neg0_5'],0)} of {M['shape']['n_journals']} journals below −0.5) with excess kurtosis {M['shape']['median_excess_kurt']:.1f}, reflecting a minority of very quick acceptances. With at least 100 visible papers per journal, shrinkage had little to add.")
P("These results have practical implications. Authors comparing journals should read turnaround as a distribution with a long right tail, prefer recent (about 12-month) data and quantiles to means, and expect an additional upward shift. Publishers that report a single median or ‘average’ should state the cohort (acceptance or submission), whether desk rejections are included, and give quantiles; Bilalli et al. (2021) and Andersen et al. (2021) show that current practice is inconsistent. Evaluators or funders who require a maximum time to acceptance as a selection criterion should recognise that no past statistic supports a guarantee at the level of the individual paper: even for the minority of journals that accepted most papers within 72 days, the realised share was lower than forecast, and a share of 0.5 or 0.8 is not a promise.")
P("The study complements the registered PubMed-wide plan of Dianovics et al. (2026) by evaluating forecasts out of time and in three publishers' journals across all fields, rather than modelling lag within a random split of biomedical articles.")
H("4.1 Limitations", 2)
for t in ["Conditioning on acceptance: only accepted papers have dates. Statements about ‘papers accepted within 72 days’ do not describe the chance that a submission is accepted at all, and rejected or withdrawn submissions, which may differ in duration, are unobserved.",
          "Publisher-defined dates: the meaning of ‘received’ is not harmonised and cannot be verified here. Some journals show medians of 10–30 days that are hard to reconcile with a full peer-review cycle and may reflect re-submission dates; findings about the fastest journals should be treated with particular caution. Attempts to check deposited dates against publisher article pages were blocked, and Crossref–PubMed agreement is not independent verification.",
          "Frame: three publishers, journals with roughly 150–3,000 dated articles per year, and journals that deposit dates. Elsevier, IEEE and other publishers are outside the frame; the PubMed check includes some of them but covers only 32 journals. Results may not generalise to small journals or to journals of other publishers.",
          "Design: two origins and six-month cohorts; the ranking among near-identical forecasters (differences below 2% of loss) is statistically detectable with 781 journals but of little practical consequence. Reference count is an after-the-fact covariate.",
          "Hypotheses H2–H5 were not supported; H2 failed its pre-specified rule narrowly, and the pre-specified Clopper–Pearson interval for H4 ignores the dependence of a journal's two origins (see the cluster bootstrap above). The pre-specified ±3-point and 5% thresholds were judgement calls made before seeing data.",
          "The frame was defined from 2023 volumes and journal-origins require at least 50 target papers; both use information that post-dates the earlier origin or the outcome, a mild selection effect. Only two confirmatory origins were used (four in exploratory checks).", "Baselines are simple: we did not evaluate richer forecasters (e.g., quantile regression on journal-level trend and size features), and the empirical-Bayes forecaster relies on a normal-theory sampling variance that is inappropriate for heavy-tailed data (excess kurtosis about 2).", "Full texts of most prior studies could not be read (abstract-level reading only), and the literature search was not systematic; probability-integral-transform histograms pool dependent journal-origins and carry no interval; the post-hoc analyses (D6–D7) were prompted by the results and are exploratory.", "No causal claim is made about why turnaround differs or drifts."]:
    p = doc.add_paragraph(t, style="List Bullet"); p.paragraph_format.space_after = Pt(3)
H("5. Conclusion")
P("Past journal-level turnaround is a useful but wide and slightly optimistic forecast of the next paper's time to acceptance. Simple empirical quantiles from the last year perform as well as more elaborate forecasters, drift is large, and statements that a journal accepts a given share of papers within a fixed number of days cannot be treated as guarantees. Reporting and evaluation practices should present turnaround as drifting distributions with explicit definitions and uncertainty.")
